//? Title: Sentence VAE: Teacher Reconstruction and Target-Free Generation
//? Subtitle: Explicit masked GRUs, Gaussian codes, previous-target prefixes and ancestral token draws
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Sentence generation turns a continuous code into a variable-length token sequence. It must
predict each next token from its own previous outputs, while reconstruction can use the true
previous tokens. This report compares both policies in a sentence VAE, using a
compact embedding 8/GRU16/code 4 model on 432 exhaustive local grammar sentences. Three epochs
perform 27 visible Adam updates; four fixed validation draws select epoch 3. Test sentence
NLL 18.650350 plus KL 0.030573 gives negative ELBO 18.680923 nats/sentence. Teacher token
perplexity 16.404448 is worse than the fitting-only unigram reference 15.689205. None of the
20 greedy or 20 token-sampled prior cases follows the grammar. Exact PAD checks, fixed-code
causality, independent noise, unfiltered examples and a complete editable C++ program make
these short-run limitations visible rather than treating teacher prediction as fluent generation.
)
\toc

> Encode the complete sentence; decode causally at fixed code

[Bowman et al. (2016)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.06349) study variational sentence models
with global continuous latent representations. This compact teaching adaptation uses a
19-word vocabulary, separate 8-dimensional encoder/decoder embeddings, GRU hidden width 16
and four Gaussian coordinates. It does not reproduce the paper's LSTM, dropout or KL-annealing
recipe. The related posterior-collapse report compares annealing explicitly; this run keeps
KL weight 1 throughout.

The encoder sees the **complete observed sentence**. At each time step it proposes a GRU
state, then retains the previous state for PAD 0. EOS 2 is a normal nonzero input. Two linear
heads produce mu and log variance; explicit Gaussian epsilon forms z=mu+exp(lv/2)*epsilon.
The decoder begins at tanh(dense(z)), uses its own embedding/GRU, and shares a 19-token output
projection across all seven steps. There is no direct encoder-state or target-token bypass.

For batch size B and length T, the two GRUs require O(B T H(E+H)) arithmetic,
and the vocabulary projection adds O(B T H V), with embedding width E, hidden
width H and vocabulary size V. Recurrent steps run sequentially in T. Training
retains the time-step activations for BPTT; its storage grows linearly in B and T
for this fixed architecture. The short run below fixes T=7, E=8, H=16 and V=19.

